/*******************************************************************************
	Kant in kant: the plan
*******************************************************************************/
Goal: jitted turtles all the way down. rule() fires its parse; the parse fires
its action; the action BUILDS an instruct and returns it as its label. Running
the label runs the program. Proof that kant is a language that writes itself.

/*******************************************************************************
	Two phases, never mixed
*******************************************************************************/
Build time: a rule action fires during the parse and builds an instruct. It
evaluates nothing (bear-trap #34: building must never fire).
The one exception: code that DECIDES the parse (accept or reject a match) runs
at parse time, because the parse cannot wait for it.
Until kant can write a rejection (open question 1), a rule's parse decision
may live on its own small rule beneath it, in C++ -- the two-step. The worked
example, as it stood until SEQ 469:
    SHORTCUTs=[-+~`$_:,]+ ruleMethod=shortcutTest;   decides (rejects ++ -- $$)
    SHORTCUt  shortcut=SHORTCUTs;                    builds (kant action)
SEQ 469 dropped the decision on the new road instead of moving it: `,` already
separates a shortcut from an operator in a print line (Tony), so ++ is two
shortcuts there. SHORTCUt is one rule again, with no C++:
    SHORTCUt  shortcut+=[-+~`$_:,];                  builds (kant action)
The + on the label reads as [set]+ (one label per run, traced) and is inert:
no row tells it from shortcut=[...], since shortcuts printed one by one print
what the run prints. The old road keeps its refusal (shortcutTest stays for
aCTionShortcuT). The two-step pattern stands for the next real decider.
Run time: the instruct runs. testOP is every instruct's method: if a field has
a method, run it; if not, hand it back as is. A plain value is a valid chain end.

/*******************************************************************************
	The shapes
*******************************************************************************/
Expression chain   op target argument, a tree (EXPRESSIOn builds these today). Each element in the chain is an instruction (an instruct), and target and/or argument can be an instruction, hence the chain. When an instruction fires, it first checks its argument and if argument is an instruction, it fires the argument; when the argument returns; the target is checked and goes thru the same process. When both argument and target are resolved, their results are passed to the op method to produce a result. Every instruction has a method, testOP, that runs its op method on argument and target once they are resolved.
seq instruct       op = seq, argument = a LIST of statement instructs.
                   opSeq walks the list in order. An action body is a seq.
                   Built.
print instruct     op = printList, argument = the list of items. A shortcut
                   item is its own instruct, op = printShortcut. Built.
if instruct        op = ifThen, argument = [condition, then, else?]. opIf runs
                   the condition and one arm, or none. Built (SEQ 473).
loop instructs     whileLoop [condition, statement], doLoop [statement,
                   condition], forLoop [cursor, source, statement, <-?]; each op
                   catches break and continue. Built (SEQ 478 / 480 / 482).
iterate instruct   op = iterateOn, argument = [cursor, source, attributes 0/1,
                   members 0/1]; opIterate calls iterateSetup, the setup both
                   roads share. WHILe and ++ / -- do the walking. Built (486).
branch instruct    op = branchSignal, argument = the keyword. opBranch sets
                   the signal (break 1, continue 2). return refuses by name
                   until action bodies exist (rung 5). Built (SEQ 476).
block instruct     a { } builds a seq (a list of statements), as STARt does. Built (SEQ 476).
Every value-producing instruct hands back its result on its own node, never a
shared slot. Today's exceptions, each on purpose:
    opPrintList ends in opPrint and hands back trueResult (the shared sentinel);
    opSeq hands back its argument list;
    opPrintShortcut hands back null, so the print loop skips it as an item;
    opIf hands back the arm's result, or nothing when no arm ran (R3, the
    yield ruling); the loop ops, opBranch and opIterate hand back nothing.

/*******************************************************************************
	Branches: signals, not targets
*******************************************************************************/
break, continue, return raise a signal (branchKind). Nobody computes a target.
The nearest loop instruct reads break and continue after each statement;
the action's own seq reads return.
DESIGN RULE, "the landing finds itself" (Tony, 2026-10-30): each op declares
which signals it catches -- break and continue are caught by the nearest loop,
return by the nearest action boundary. A branch knows nothing of where it lands.
IF and seq pass signals through: a seq stops its list when a signal is set and
leaves it set (it clears before each statement, as the old road's block does);
an if runs one arm and hands the signal on. A stray break with no loop ends its
seq and the next statement runs (SEQ 477 R0).

/*******************************************************************************
	Frames
*******************************************************************************/
processAction keeps its bracket: each activation's locals saved and restored.
Only its body walk changes, to "run the chain". The one engine change the
plan needs, beside the new ops (opSeq, opIf, opWhile, opDo, opFor, opBranch,
opIterate -- all built). This is rung 5, and return waits on it (whileT WH-8).

/*******************************************************************************
	The new grammar
*******************************************************************************/
A new incantation beside grammar (kantGrammar). How it is reached today: a file
registers it in fILEs and includes it (printFormatT's prelude is the pattern),
and a new rule runs by being called by name, rule("text") (R6). Swapping
Grokking out of the search list changes name lookups, not the driver (seal 234),
so that swap is dropped for now.
Setup, the bootstrap and anything already compiled stay on Grokking. No change
to existing code or interpreter.
Every new rule action is kant and returns an instruct, except a parse decision
(see Two phases).
Rules for every new rule:
    - No meaning carried in C++ flags. If an item means something, that meaning
      is an instruct, an op or an attribute, never a bit the engine remembers.
    - Resolve operands at run time, never at build.
    - A built instruct holds copies (copyOf), never the action's own locals: a
      local is per-call scratch, so an instruct that kept one would read the
      last call's value (bear-trap 50; PRINt's itemsMint, SHORTCUt's scText).
    - A parse decision cannot share a rule with the build: ruleMethod= on a
      rule displaces its code={} (measured, SEQ 462). The decider goes on its
      own small rule beneath (the two-step; SHORTCUTs was the example until
      SEQ 469 dropped its decision).
    - A list is known by its TAG, never by isLIST / isLisT. Adjacent operands
      (qa qb) are one operand, a list tagged ADJACENt that EXPRESSIOn's action
      hands up; PRINt adds its members as separate items (SEQ 467 / 468).

/*******************************************************************************
	The ladder: one rung at a time, each checked against the old grammar
*******************************************************************************/
Every rung runs the same incantation under both grammars and compares output.
    1  two prints in a seq             statements in a chain        DONE
    2  EXPRESSIOn (EXPRESSIOn, R0)    expressions in the new grammar  DONE
       PRINt finished alongside: formats, shortcuts and adjacent
       expressions DONE (SEQ 468 / 469); left: the quoted-print row (R7)
    3  IF                              build an if instruct; then / else  DONE
    4  the loops: WHILe, Do, FOr,      seq + branch signals: break,       DONE
       iterate; break / continue;      continue; blocks build a seq
       blocks
       STATE 2026-10-30: all built and bought. The shape -- a kant action
       that only BUILDS, plus a small C++ op that runs -- held for every one.
       defer is off the new road's control rules (If, BASICELSe, Do, WHILe,
       FOr, BRANCh, BLOCk, ITERATe); ELSEIf keeps it while it binds the old
       road's aCTionIF. NEXT is rung 5.
    5  an action, called and self-called    the frame bracket      NEXT
                                       (return lands here; WH-8 waits)
    6  the jitter walks the chain      later

/*******************************************************************************
	Jitting
*******************************************************************************/
The list is how the program is held, not how it runs. Interpreted: testOP walks
the list. Jitted: the jitter walks it once at compile time and emits straight-
line code; seq flattens, if becomes a branch, a loop a back-edge, break a jump.
First step, before any emitter: a recon of the new ops under jitter;, measured
against the rules for new rules. Today the new ops hand over to run time.
A split rule (decide below, build above) adds one label level; check it there.

/*******************************************************************************
	Open questions
*******************************************************************************/
1  A kant spelling for a rule action that rejects a match. Today only a null
   from a C++ action fails a match; no kant spelling produces one. SHORTCUt
   no longer needs it (SEQ 469 dropped its decision); the question stands for
   the next rule that must decide.
2  Two-part rule actions on one rule: a parse action (decides, may reject) and
   a runtime action (builds). One rule cannot carry ruleMethod= and code={}
   today. First read what parseAction / parseCondition already do.
   Read 2026-10-29: parseAction (Generate.rtn) runs a rule's C++ actor
   (builtinActoR) AS its parse, and a null from it fails the term -- a
   parse-time decide/reject slot, but C++ only and generated-parser only.
   parseCondition consumes nothing and matches on its min alone. Both carry
   "UNPINNED, no road reaches it": neither is tested, and neither lets a kant
   action reject (that is question 1). A row comes before anything leans on them.
3  Is a rule's decision always separable from its build? Watched through IF,
   the loops, break / continue, blocks and iterate (SEQ 473-486): none needed a
   parse decision -- each action only builds and refuses by name when a child
   hands up nothing. Still open in general (ELSEIf, action bodies).
4  Adjacent expressions -- SETTLED SEQ 468. EXPRESSIOn does not stop: like the
   old road, adjacent operands are ONE list value, but known by the tag
   ADJACENt (the old road's split is C++: interpretXPaccum's isLIST, SEQ 465).
   print qa qb:; prints 2 3 on both roads. An ADJACENt list meeting ANY
   operator (call and subscript included) refuses by name, "not built on the
   new road yet". What remains is question 5.
5  List-taking operators (Tony wants more than +%; which ones not yet named),
   PARKED with two shapes (SEQ 467): (A) one C++ helper isListArg (isLIST, or
   the tag ADJACENt) called by each of today's twelve list arms -- one call per
   new operator, but a kantGrammar tag spelled in C++; (B) EXPRESSIOn spreads
   the list at build, one instruct per member, for any op whose Operators
   entry carries takesList -- no C++, one attribute per new operator, but the
   value becomes the seq's, +%'s holder unwrap has no list to act on, and the
   spread sits in the slot code SEQ 448 R6 wants simplified. Tony, 2026-10-30:
   leave the REFUSED on an adjacent run in place (print items separate with ,).
6  Build order vs branch targets (2026-10-30). Children build first, so a
   break cannot name its loop -- the loop does not exist yet when the break is
   built. Only the loop could bind its breaks, by walking its own body at build
   (a build-time walk that NO HUNT would have to allow). Today the signal finds
   its landing at run time and nothing binds. Open until new-road loops jit,
   where a break wants a jump target.
/*******************************************************************************
	Standing rulings for the new grammar (refreshed 2026-10-29)
*******************************************************************************/
R0  New-grammar rules are spelled UPPERCASe: uppercase, last letter lowercase
    (EXPRESSIOn, STATEMENt, PRINt, If, Do).                     (SEQ 431)
R1  TOKENize, DEFINing and PRINTing stay as they are, shared with the old
    grammar; no new rule reuses their exact spellings.          (SEQ 431)
R2  Arguments with no operator are carried as a list first. No new link field
    (nextInParent) unless a list is shown not to do the job.    (SEQ 431)
R3  IF's shape: one instruct in its parent's seq. Its method runs the
    condition, runs one arm, and hands back the arm's result, or nothing when
    no arm ran.                                                 (SEQ 431, 473)
    Operand layout SETTLED (SEQ 472 R2): op ifThen, the argument a list
    [condition, then, else?]. The loops follow it.
R4  Expression tiers (tester's EXPRESSIOn, tier-split 920d1a81): arithmetic
    folds at once; compare, logic and assign each wait in their own slot; a
    looser operator closes every tighter slot first; the same tier closes its
    own, so a tier reads left to right; assign never closes assign -- a second
    assign nests, right to left. The end closes tightest first. testerChainT
    pins it.                                                    (SEQ 435 R2)
R5  Chained assign is accepted: a = b = c assigns right to left (TC-16).
                                                                (SEQ 435 R1)
R6  Bottom-up: each new rule is tested by calling it by name, rule("text").
    The search-list swap is dropped for now.                    (SEQ 438 R0)
R7  An action reads its component labels, builds an instruct in tester's
    layout (op [1], target [2], argument [3], method testOP) and returns it.
    Nothing runs inside an action.                              (SEQ 438 R1)
R8  New ops are C++ first, then kant. Each scaffold carries a dated TODO line,
    "retires when the kant action of the same name passes the same rows",
    retired by mapping, groups.ext line included. opSeq, opPrintList and
    opPrintShortcut are such scaffolds. shortcutTest was the new grammar's C++
    parse decider; since SEQ 469 only the old road calls it.   (SEQ 438 R4)
Shared with the old grammar, referenced never redefined: TOKENize, DEFINing,
    PRINTing, ANYtoken, followedBy; the C++ bootstrap seeds NamE, NumbeR,
    QuotE, SemI, GrouP, define, counter; Operators and its UnaryOPS bin.
Parked: fire(label), a command that fires a rule's action on a label with no
    parse. Returns only if a rung needs parse-then-fire.        (SEQ 440)
